---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import { breadcrumbSchema, graph, organizationSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Editorial policy', href: '/editorial-policy/' },
];

const schema = graph([
  organizationSchema(),
  breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbs),
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Editorial policy',
    url: `${SITE.url}/editorial-policy/`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#website` },
  },
]);
---

<PageLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How HostScope decides what to publish, how we handle corrections, and the rules that keep commercial interests out of our recommendations."
  eyebrow="Editorial standards"
  canonical={`${SITE.url}/editorial-policy/`}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schema={schema}
  updated={new Date('2026-09-10')}
>
  <p class="lead text-[1.125rem] text-ink-700">
    HostScope is funded by affiliate commission, which means our editorial independence is not
    something readers should take on trust. It should be checkable. This page sets out the rules we
    work to and how you can verify them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="independence">Editorial independence</h2>

  <p>
    No commercial partner has any input into what we publish. Specifically:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Hostinger does not see articles before publication.</li>
    <li>Hostinger cannot request that a criticism be removed or softened.</li>
    <li>We do not accept payment for a review, a score, or a placement in a "best of" list.</li>
    <li>We do not run sponsored posts, advertorials or paid guest content.</li>
    <li>Commission rates do not influence which plan we recommend.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That last point is the one readers are most sceptical about, so it is worth being concrete:
    Hostinger's affiliate payout is a percentage of the sale, and the percentage does not differ
    meaningfully between Premium at $2.99/mo and Cloud Enterprise at $29.99/mo. Recommending the
    cheapest suitable plan does not cost us money. Recommending the wrong plan costs us the reader.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-publish">What we will and will not publish</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>We publish</th>
        <th>We do not publish</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Renewal prices on every plan</td>
        <td>Introductory prices presented as the ongoing cost</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Published resource limits, including the unflattering ones</td>
        <td>Marketing feature lists copied from a vendor page</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Explicit "do not buy this" guidance per plan</td>
        <td>Blanket claims that one host is best for everyone</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Comparisons where a competitor wins rows</td>
        <td>Comparisons engineered so our preferred host wins everything</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Corrections with a visible note</td>
        <td>Silent edits that hide an earlier mistake</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy and sourcing</h2>

  <p>
    Every price, specification and limit we publish comes from one of three places: Hostinger's public
    pricing pages, Hostinger's own technical documentation, or our own direct observation. Where we
    cite a number we record the date it was checked.
  </p>

  <p>
    We do not publish statistics we cannot source. We do not invent customer quotes, survey results or
    benchmark figures. If a claim in an article cannot be traced to a source or to our own testing, it
    should not be there — and if you find one, please tell us.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">Use of AI</h2>

  <p>
    We use AI tooling for research assistance, outline generation and copy editing. Every article is
    reviewed by a named human editor before publication, and every factual claim about pricing or
    specifications is checked against source data by that editor. We do not publish machine-generated
    text that has not been verified by a person.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections policy</h2>

  <p>
    When we get something wrong we fix it, and where the error was material we add a note. To request
    a correction, email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.publisher.email}`}>{SITE.publisher.email}</a> with the page URL and what is
    incorrect. We aim to respond within two business days.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Factual error in a price or specification:</strong> corrected immediately, with a note.</li>
    <li><strong>Outdated information:</strong> updated, with the "last updated" date refreshed.</li>
    <li><strong>Disagreement with an opinion:</strong> not a correction. We will consider publishing a counter-argument.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="comments">Reader contact</h2>

  <p>
    We read everything sent to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.publisher.email}`}>{SITE.publisher.email}</a>. Corrections, factual
    challenges and "this did not work for me" reports are all genuinely useful — they are how a site
    like this stays accurate when the underlying product changes every few months.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
